7.5.23 purityfiltration_lib
---------------------------

Status: experimental 
*Library:*
     purityfiltration.lib

*Purpose:*
     Algorithms for computing a purity filtration of a given module

*Authors:*
     Christian Schilli, christian.schilli@rwth-aachen.de
     Viktor Levandovskyy, levandov@math.rwth-aachen.de

*Overview:*
     Purity is a notion with several meanings. In our context it is
     equidimensionality
     of a module (that is all M is pure iff any nonzero submodule of N
     has the same dimension as N).
     Notably, one should define purity with respect to a given
     dimension function. In the context
     of this library the corresponding function is the homological
     grade number j_A(M) of a module M over
     an K-algebra A. j_A(M) is the minimal integer k, such that
     Ext^k_A(M,A) != 0.

*References:*
     [AQ] Alban Quadrat: Grade filtration of linear functional systems,
     INRIA Report 7769 (2010), to appear in Acta Applicanda Mathematica.
     [B93] Jan-Erik Bjoerk: Analytic D-modules and applications, Kluwer
     Acad. Publ., 1993.
     [MB10] Mohamed Barakat: Purity Filtration and the Fine Structure
     of Autonomy. Proc. MTNS, 2010.


*Procedures:*

* Menu:

* projectiveDimension:: compute a shortest resolution of coker(T) and its projective dimension
* purityFiltration:: compute the purity filtration of coker(R)
* purityTriang:: compute a triangular blockmatrix T, such that coker(R) isomorphic to coker(T)
* gradeNumber:: gives the grade number of the module coker(R)
* showgrades:: gives all grade numbers of the modules represented by the elements of T
* allExtOfLeft:: computes all right ext-modules ext^i(M,D) of a left module M=coker(R) over the ring D
* allExtOfRight:: computes all left ext-modules ext^i(M,D) of a right module M=coker(R) over the ring D
* doubleExt:: computes the left module ext^i(ext^i(M,D),D) over the ring D, M=coker(R)
* allDoubleExt:: computes all double ext modules ext^i(ext^j(M,D),D) of the left module coker(R) over the ring D
* is_pure:: checks whether the module coker(R) is pure
* purelist:: checks whether all the modules represented by the elements of T are pure


