D.2.13.2 killTask
.................

Procedure from library `tasks.lib' (*note tasks_lib::).

*Usage:*
     killTask(t), t task

*Return:*
     nothing. If the state of t is 'started', then t is stopped first.
     The internal data structures of t are erased and its state is set
     to 'uninitialized'.

*Note_*
     'killTask(t);' is not the same as 'kill t;'. The latter command
     does not erase the internal data structures of t. Hence killTask()
     should be called for any no longer needed task in order to free
     memory.

*Example:*
     LIB "tasks.lib";
     ring R = 0, (x,y), dp;
     ideal I = x9y2+x10, x2y7-y8;
     task t = "std", list(I);
     startTasks(t);
     t;
     ==> A task with the following properties:
     ==> command:          std
     ==> no. of arguments: 1
     ==> state:            started
     ==>
     killTask(t);
     t;
     ==> An uninitialized task
     ==>
     getState(t);
     ==> uninitialized

* Menu:

See also:
* createTask::
* getState::
* printTask::
* stopTask::


