     intmat2binomials(intmat M);

*Return:*
     an ideal generated by the binomials which correspond to the
     exponent vectors given by the rows of `M'

*Note_*
     The number of variables in the basering `nvars(basering)' has to
     be at least the number of columns `ncols(M)', otherwise the
     function exits with an error is thrown (see *note ERROR::).  The
     vector with all entries zero represents 1.

*Example:*
     LIB "normaliz.lib";
     ring S = 37,(u,v,w,x,y,z),dp;
     intmat M[2][6] = 1,0,1,-1,-2,0,  -3,4,5,0,0,1;
     intmat2binomials(M);
     ==> _[1]=-xy2+uw
     ==> _[2]=v4w5z-u3

* Menu:

See also:
* binomials2intmat::


