D.7.4.6 invar
.............

Procedure from library `invar.lib' (*note invar_lib::).

*Usage:*
     invar(<matrix>)

*Returns:*
     If m is a n x n matrix with coefficients in the ring 'group',
     representing the action on some vector space V, then invar(m);
     gives polynomials in x(1),x(2),...,x(n) who generate the invariant
     ring. The following global variables will be set:
     polyring of type <ring> polynomial ring in x(1),...,x(n) invring
     of type <ideal> entries generate the inv. ring representation of
     type <matrix>
     The base ring will be set to 'polyring' which is a global variable
     representing the polynomial ring on which the group acts.  The
     variable 'representation' will be set to the input m.

*Example:*
     LIB "invar.lib";
     SL(2);                          // Take the group SL_2
     matrix m=dsum(SLrep,SLrep,SLrep,SLrep);
     // 4 copies of the standard representation
     invar(m);                       // empirical evidence for FFT
