D.12.3.8 semidiv
................

Procedure from library `hyperel.lib' (*note hyperel_lib::).

*Usage:*
     semidiv(D,h,f);

*Return:*
     list P

*Note_*
     important: Divisor D has to be semireduced!
     Computes semireduced divisor P[1][3]*(P[1][1], P[1][2]) +...+
     P[size(P)][3]* *(P[size(P)][1], P[size(P)][2]) -
     (*)infty=div(D[1],D[2])
     Curve C:y^2+h(x)y=f(x) is defined over basering.

*Example:*
     LIB "hyperel.lib";
     ring R=7,x,dp;
     // hyperelliptic curve y^2 + h*y = f
     poly h=x;
     poly f=x5+5x4+6x2+x+3;
     // Divisor
     list D=x2-1,2x-1;
     semidiv(D,h,f);
     ==> [1]:
     ==>    [1]:
     ==>       -1
     ==>    [2]:
     ==>       -3
     ==>    [3]:
     ==>       1
     ==> [2]:
     ==>    [1]:
     ==>       1
     ==>    [2]:
     ==>       1
     ==>    [3]:
     ==>       1


File: singular.info,  Node: cantoradd,  Next: cantorred,  Prev: semidiv,  Up: hyperel_lib

D.12.3.9 cantoradd
..................

Procedure from library `hyperel.lib' (*note hyperel_lib::).

*Usage:*
     cantoradd(D,Q,h,f);

*Return:*
     list P

*Note_*
     Cantor's Algorithm - composition
     important: D and Q have to be semireduced!
     Computes semireduced divisor div(P[1],P[2])= div(D[1],D[2]) +
     div(Q[1],Q[2]) The divisors are defined over the basering.
     Curve C: y^2+h(x)y=f(x) is defined over the basering.

*Example:*
     LIB "hyperel.lib";
     ring R=7,x,dp;
     // hyperelliptic curve y^2 + h*y = f
     poly h=x;
     poly f=x5+5x4+6x2+x+3;
     // two divisors in rational representation
     list D=x2-1,2x-1;
     list Q=x2-3x+2,-3x+1;
     cantoradd(D,Q,h,f);
     ==> [1]:
     ==>    x2-x-2
     ==> [2]:
     ==>    -3x+1


File: singular.info,  Node: cantorred,  Next: double,  Prev: cantoradd,  Up: hyperel_lib

D.12.3.10 cantorred
...................

Procedure from library `hyperel.lib' (*note hyperel_lib::).

*Usage:*
     cantorred(D,h,f);

*Return:*
     list N

*Note_*
     Cantor's algorithm - reduction.
     important: Divisor D has to be semireduced!
     Computes reduced divisor div(N[1],N[2])= div(D[1],D[2]).
     The divisors are defined over the basering.
     Curve C: y^2+h(x)y=f(x) is defined over the basering.

*Example:*
     LIB "hyperel.lib";
     ring R=7,x,dp;
     // hyperelliptic curve y^2 + h*y = f
     poly h=x;
     poly f=x5+5x4+6x2+x+3;
     // semireduced divisor
     list D=2x4+3x3-3x-2, -x3-2x2+3x+1;
     cantorred(D,h,f);
     ==> [1]:
     ==>    x2-2x+2
     ==> [2]:
     ==>    2x-2


File: singular.info,  Node: double,  Next: cantormult,  Prev: cantorred,  Up: hyperel_lib

D.12.3.11 double
................

Procedure from library `hyperel.lib' (*note hyperel_lib::).

*Usage:*
     double(D,h,f);

*Return:*
     list Q=2*D

*Note_*
     important: Divisor D has to be semireduced!
     Special case of Cantor's algorithm.
     Computes reduced divisor div(Q[1],Q[2])= 2*div(D[1],D[2]).
     The divisors are defined over the basering.
     Curve C:y^2+h(x)y=f(x) is defined over the basering.

*Example:*
     LIB "hyperel.lib";
     ring R=7,x,dp;
     // hyperelliptic curve y^2 + h*y = f
     poly h=x;
     poly f=x5+5x4+6x2+x+3;
     // reduced divisor
     list D=x2-1,2x-1;
     double(D,h,f);
     ==> [1]:
     ==>    x2-2x+1
     ==> [2]:
     ==>    3x-2


File: singular.info,  Node: cantormult,  Prev: double,  Up: hyperel_lib

D.12.3.12 cantormult
....................

Procedure from library `hyperel.lib' (*note hyperel_lib::).

*Usage:*
     cantormult(m,D,h,f);

*Return:*
     list res=m*D

*Note_*
     important: Divisor D has to be semireduced!
     Uses repeated doublings for a faster computation
     of the reduced divisor m*D.
     Attention: Factor m=int, this means bounded.
     For m<0 the inverse of m*D is returned.
     The divisors are defined over the basering.
     Curve C: y^2+h(x)y=f(x) is defined over the basering.

*Example:*
     LIB "hyperel.lib";
     ring R=7,x,dp;
     // hyperelliptic curve y^2 + h*y = f
     poly h=x;
     poly f=x5+5x4+6x2+x+3;
     // reduced divisor
     list D=x2-1,2x-1;
     cantormult(34,D,h,f);
     ==> [1]:
     ==>    x2-3x-3
     ==> [2]:
     ==>    x+1


