5.2 Parallel computing
======================

Parallel computing in SINGULAR
******************************

The available ressources are given by the number of cpus (set by the
command line parameter `--cpus=..', or the environment variable
`SINGULAR_CPUS', defaults to the number of processors currently online)
and the number of threads (set by the command line parameter
`--threads=..', defaults to 1).  Threads are currently used with FLINT
and NTL.
Setting `--cpus' to 0 disables the creation of new processes and
chooses a sequentiell algorithm if possible.
Using parallel algorithms can reduce the overall computing time, by can
also increase the memory usage by a factor given by the number of used
cpu cores.

Via modular methods, requires QQ as coefficient field:
   * assPrimes: *note assPrimes::

   * chinrem: *note chinrem::

   * farey: *note farey::

   * locNormal: *note locNormal::

   * modNormal: *note modNormal::

   * modPrimdecGTZ: *note modPrimdecGTZ::, sequentiell: *note
     primdecGTZ::

   * modQuotient: *note modQuotient::, sequentiell: *note quotient::

   * modSat: *note modSat::, sequentiell: *note sat::

   * modStd: *note modStd::, sequentiell: *note std::, see also: *note
     groebner::

   * pdf: *note pfd::

   * syModStd: *note syModStd::

   * system("verifyGB",I): *note system::

   * zeroRadical: *note zeroRadical::

Via modular methods, requires ZZ as coefficient domain:
   * primdecZ: *note primdecZ::

Via modular methods, requires a function field over QQ as coefficient
field:
   * ffmodStd: *note ffmodStd::

Via modular methods, requires a algebraic field over QQ as coefficient
field:
   * nfmodStd: *note nfmodStd::

   * nfmodSyz: *note nfmodSyz::

Via different algorithms:
   * primdec: *note primdec::

   * minAss: *note minAss::

   * signatureNemethi: *note signatureNemethi::

Frameworks for parallel algorithms:
   * for modular methods: *note modular_lib::

   * for different algorithms at once: *note watchdog::


